      "## When a portfolio should become a full business site",
      "Most portfolios start as a simple showcase, and that is right. But there comes a point when the portfolio needs to become a full business website, and it is worth recognising that point before you outgrow the site. The first sign is that you are selling something as well as showing it, such as prints, presets, templates or a course, and you need a shop rather than a contact form. The second sign is that clients need to book you, and you need a booking system with deposits rather than back-and-forth emails. The third sign is that you need a client area, where clients can view proofs, approve work or download files. The fourth sign is that your content has grown, with a blog, guides or a newsletter that brings in clients through search, and the simple site cannot organise it properly. Any one of these is web app work, starting from $4,999, and it is better to plan for it than to keep adding features to a site that was never built for them. The good news is that a well-built portfolio grows naturally into a business site, because the case studies, the about page and the contact flow are the same. What changes is the system behind them, not the story they tell.",
