      "For a gym or fitness studio, the website is the front desk that works while the trainers are on the floor. People search for a gym online first, compare memberships and class times on their phones, and judge the gym by its photos and how easy it is to start a trial. A weak website does not just look dated, it sends a potential member to the gym down the road whose site lets them join in minutes. This guide explains gym website cost in plain terms: what the site has to do, what memberships, class timetables and trainer profiles add to the price, and what a good gym website should cost in 2026.",
      "## The short answer",
      "A professional marketing website for a gym starts from $1,499 with CINEM. That covers a fast, mobile-first site with membership plans and prices, the class timetable, trainer profiles, trial sign-ups, photos and clear ways to contact the gym. A fuller site with online membership sign-up, class booking, member accounts and payments is web app work and starts from $4,999. If you also want a WhatsApp or web chatbot to answer questions and take trial bookings around the clock, that starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month, and an AI calling agent for missed calls starts from $1,499 setup plus $399 per month. The honest rule is simple. Pay for the site that turns searches into trial sign-ups and members, not for features members will never use. If you want the wider picture on website pricing, our related post How much does a website cost in 2026? An honest breakdown explains the same pricing logic across business types, and our related post Booking website cost: salons, clinics and hotels explains the booking side in more detail.",
      "## What a gym website must do",
      "Before you compare prices, be clear about the job. A gym website has to do six things well. First, it must show the gym, with real photos and video of the space, the equipment and the classes, because people join a gym they can picture themselves in. Second, it must show membership plans with honest prices, so a visitor knows what joining costs before they arrive. Third, it must show the class timetable clearly, with times, trainers and how to book a place. Fourth, it must make starting easy, with a trial sign-up or a free first class that takes less than a minute to book. Fifth, it must build trust, with trainer profiles, member reviews and results that show the gym works for people like the visitor. Sixth, it must work perfectly on a phone, because almost every visitor will be on a phone, often comparing two or three gyms at once. A site that does these six things will outperform a prettier site that does not, every time.",
      "## Memberships, pricing and joining online",
      "Memberships are the heart of a gym website, and how they are presented decides much of the cost. The simplest version is a clear membership page with each plan, its price, what is included, and a WhatsApp button or a short form to join. The next step is online sign-up, where a visitor picks a plan, fills in their details, and pays the first month online. The fuller version is a member system: members manage their own plan, book classes, freeze a membership, and pay automatically each month. That is web app work, and it is where the price rises, because the system has to handle payments, renewals, cancellations and member records without mistakes. Prices should be shown honestly. A gym that hides its membership prices does not get more enquiries, it gets fewer, because visitors assume the worst and choose a gym that is upfront with them. If a price depends on the plan or the contract length, say so and show the options clearly. A trial offer deserves its own attention. A free first class or a one-week trial is the strongest way to turn a website visitor into a member, and the sign-up for it should be the easiest thing on the whole site. A WhatsApp or web chatbot from $999 setup plus $299 per month can answer the common questions instantly, such as the membership prices, the class times, or whether a trial is available, and pass serious enquiries to the gym.",
      "## Class timetables and trainer profiles",
      "The class timetable is the page gym members visit most, and it is worth doing properly. A good timetable shows the classes for the week, the time, the trainer, the level, and how many places are left, and it lets a member book in one tap. A timetable that is a photo of a printed sheet, or a PDF that has to be downloaded, tells a visitor the gym does not keep its information up to date. Trainer profiles are the other essential content. People join a trainer, not just a gym, and a short profile with a photo, specialities, qualifications and a human introduction builds confidence before the first visit. Member stories complete the picture. A short story from a real member, with a photo and what changed for them, does far more than any claim the gym makes about itself. None of this is expensive to build, but it does need someone at the gym to keep it fresh, with new photos after every great class and a timetable that is always accurate. When you budget for a gym website, budget a little time each week for photos, timetable updates and member stories, because a site that is never updated slowly stops working as a trust-builder.",
      "## Cost by type of gym website",
      "The price depends on the type of site. A simple gym website, with membership plans and prices, the class timetable, trainer profiles, trial sign-ups and reviews, is a marketing website and starts from $1,499. A gym website with online membership sign-up, class booking, member accounts and payments is web app work and starts from $4,999. A full gym system, with memberships, class bookings, payments, member accounts, trainer schedules and automated reminders, is custom software, typically $7,999 to $15,000 depending on scope. A chatbot for enquiries and trial bookings starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month, and an AI calling agent for missed calls starts from $1,499 setup plus $399 per month. On top of the build, budget for photography, because photos of the gym, the classes and the trainers are what visitors look at first, and for content, because new classes, offers and member stories are what keep the site alive. A mobile app starts from $4,999, but most gyms do not need one to begin with, because members find gyms through Google and social media, not through an app they have never heard of.",
      "## Ongoing costs",
      "A gym website is not a one-off purchase, and it is honest to plan for the running costs. Membership plans, class times, trainers and opening hours change, and someone has to keep them accurate. If the site has class booking or membership payments, those features need monitoring, because a broken booking form means empty classes. Hosting, domain renewal, security and backups are the baseline. Then there is content: new class photos, offers, challenges and member stories, which are what keep the site ranking on Google and keep visitors coming back. Many gyms handle this with a care plan. At CINEM, Care retainers are $499, $1,499 or $3,999 per month, depending on how much updating, support and ongoing improvement you need. A small studio with stable classes may only need the lower level. A busy gym with online bookings, memberships and regular content will need more. If you run ads for new members, our Lead Generation service is $599 per month plus ad spend, and our Social Media service is $499 per month for gyms that want their classes and member stories promoted properly.",
      "## How a gym website pays for itself",
      "A gym website pays for itself in three ways. The first is new members. Every member who joins through your own website is a member you earned directly, not one that came through a marketplace that takes a commission. A clear trial sign-up with instant answers converts far more searches into members than a site that makes people call during working hours only. The second is fewer empty classes and less phone time. Online class booking keeps classes full, and clear membership and timetable information means the front desk spends less time answering the same questions. The third is trust and reputation. Real photos, genuine reviews, clear prices and trainer profiles make your gym the one people recommend to friends, and that reputation brings new members month after month. The key is to measure it. Track how many trial sign-ups and memberships come from the website, and how many of those become long-term members. When you can see those numbers, the website stops being a cost and becomes a clear source of members, and the decision to invest in a better one becomes easy.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "How much does a gym website cost?",
      "A professional marketing website for a gym starts from $1,499 with CINEM. A fuller site with online membership sign-up, class booking and member accounts starts from $4,999 as a web app. A full gym system with payments and automated reminders typically costs $7,999 to $15,000. A chatbot for enquiries and trial bookings starts from $999 setup plus $299 per month.",
      "Should a gym website show membership prices?",
      "Yes. Visitors expect prices to be clear, and hiding them creates fewer sign-ups, not more. Show each plan, its price, what is included, and what changes with the contract length. Visitors do not expect prices to be low, they expect them to be honest.",
      "Do people really join online, or do they prefer to visit first?",
      "Most people do both. Many prefer to book a trial online, especially outside opening hours, and many still visit to see the gym before they commit. A good gym website offers both: an easy trial sign-up for those who want it, and a clear call or WhatsApp button for those who prefer to talk.",
      "Is a mobile app better than a website for a gym?",
      "For most gyms, no. Members find gyms through Google and social media, and they expect a fast website that works on a phone. A mobile app makes sense later, for class bookings and member check-ins, once the website is working well. A mobile app starts from $4,999, so it is better spent on getting the website right first.",
      "What are the ongoing costs after launch?",
      "Budget for hosting, domain renewal, security, membership and timetable updates, and content such as class photos and member stories. Care retainers at CINEM are $499, $1,499 or $3,999 per month depending on the level of support. A chatbot for enquiries and trial bookings is available from $999 setup plus $299 per month, and an AI calling agent from $1,499 setup plus $399 per month.",
      "If your gym website is not bringing you trial sign-ups and members, it is worth an honest look at why. Tell CINEM at hello@cinem.tech how many members you have, what happens to an enquiry today, and what visitors ask most often. We will scope the right build, from a fast marketing website to a full member system with bookings and payments, and price it honestly with the running costs included."
