      "## What a real estate website must do",
      "Before you compare prices, be clear about the job. A real estate website has to do six things well. First, it must show listings beautifully, with large photos, clear prices, floor plans and the details buyers actually filter on: bedrooms, bathrooms, area, price and location. Second, it must make search effortless, with filters that work on a phone and results that load fast. Third, it must make contact easy. A buyer who finds the right property should be able to enquire, call, or message on WhatsApp in one tap, without filling in a long form. Fourth, it must build trust, with agent profiles, reviews, sold properties and local knowledge that shows you know the area. Fifth, it must connect to your Google profile and your portal listings, so prices, availability and contact details always match. Sixth, it must capture the leads it generates, so no enquiry is lost when you are out on a viewing. A site that does these six things will outperform a prettier site that does not, every time.",
      "## Listings, search and portal feeds",
      "Listings are the heart of a real estate website, and how they are managed decides much of the cost. A small agency with a dozen properties can manage listings by hand, adding each one through a simple admin area. A larger agency needs a feed. In many markets, listings live in a CRM or a property portal, and the website pulls them in automatically, so a price change or a new listing appears on the site without anyone retyping it. This is done through a feed or an IDX integration, and it is the part of the build that most often surprises buyers, because the feed has to be mapped to the website, photos have to be handled, and the data has to stay in sync. Search is the other half. Buyers expect to filter by price, bedrooms, area and property type, sort by newest or cheapest, and save a search so they are told when something matching appears. Map search, where buyers draw an area and see what is inside it, is the feature that most impresses buyers and most adds to the build cost. The practical advice is to start with the search your buyers actually use, and add the fancier features once the site is earning its keep.",
      "## Lead capture and WhatsApp enquiries",
