      "## Cost by type of real estate website",
      "The price depends on the type of site. A simple agent website, with a handful of listings managed by hand, agent profiles, area guides and enquiry forms, is a marketing website and starts from $1,499. An agency website with dozens of listings, a portal feed, saved searches and a proper admin area is web app work and starts from $4,999. A multi-branch agency site with branch pages, agent logins, lead routing and reporting is custom software, typically $7,999 to $15,000 depending on scope. A full property portal, where the public can list properties and the platform takes a fee, is the most expensive type of all, and our related post Marketplace website cost: what it really takes explains why two-sided platforms cost so much more than a normal website. On top of the build, budget for photography, because property photos sell the listing, and for content, because area guides and market updates are what bring buyers to your site through search. A mobile app starts from $4,999, but most agents do not need one to begin with, because buyers find properties through Google and portals, not through an app they have never heard of.",
      "## Ongoing costs",
      "A real estate website is not a one-off purchase, and it is honest to plan for the running costs. Listings change constantly, and someone has to keep them accurate: prices, availability, photos and status. If your site has a portal feed, that feed needs monitoring, because a broken feed means stale listings and lost leads. Hosting, domain renewal, security and backups are the baseline. Then there is content: new area guides, market updates and sold-property stories, which are what keep the site ranking on Google. Many agencies handle this with a care plan. At CINEM, Care retainers are $499, $1,499 or $3,999 per month, depending on how much updating, support and ongoing improvement you need. A small agency with a stable set of listings may only need the lower level. A busy agency with a feed, daily listing changes and lead follow-up will need more. If you run ads to your listings, our Lead Generation service is $599 per month plus ad spend, and our Social Media service is $499 per month for agencies that want their listings promoted properly.",
      "## How a real estate website pays for itself",
      "A real estate website pays for itself in three ways. The first is direct enquiries. Every buyer who contacts you through your own site is a lead you own, not one rented from a portal. Portals are useful for discovery, but they charge for visibility and they show your listing next to your competitors. Your own site keeps the buyer with you. The second is speed. A buyer who can search, filter and enquire in minutes is more likely to book a viewing with you than with the agent whose site made them wait or fill in a long form. The third is trust and local authority. Area guides, market updates and sold stories make you the agent who knows the area, and that reputation brings referrals and repeat business. The key is to measure it. Track how many enquiries and viewings come from the website, and how many of those become deals. When you can see those numbers, the website stops being a cost and becomes a clear source of revenue, and the decision to invest in a better one becomes easy.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
