      "The knowledge: a chatbot is only as good as what it has been taught. A bot trained on your real FAQs, price list, policies and past conversations answers like your business; a bot pointed at your homepage answers like a brochure. Building and maintaining that knowledge — adding new answers, correcting wrong ones, removing out-of-date prices — is real work, and it is the work most DIY builds quietly stop doing after the first month.",
      "The handover: the moment a chatbot cannot help, a human has to take over cleanly. That means the conversation, the customer, and everything already said must arrive in one place — WhatsApp, email, or your CRM — without the customer repeating themselves. A bot with no handover is a wall, not a receptionist, and it is the single most common reason customers hate chatbots.",
      "The maintenance: prices change, services change, and the bot has to keep up. A chatbot nobody maintains starts giving wrong answers within weeks, and wrong answers from a bot cost more trust than no answer at all. Any honest quote includes the ongoing care, or says plainly that it does not.",
      "## The hidden costs nobody puts in the quote",
      "Four costs appear after the sale, and they are worth asking about before you sign. Per-message and per-conversation fees: some platforms charge a low monthly fee and then bill per conversation, which looks cheap at 100 conversations and is not cheap at 3,000. Ask for the price at your real volume, not at the trial volume.",
      "Channel fees: WhatsApp Business charges per conversation through the official API, and the rate depends on the type of message and the country. A WhatsApp chatbot quote that never mentions Meta's conversation fees is incomplete. The fees are small per message, but they are real, and they scale with success.",
